# Normalise line endings so a fresh Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true on the
# dev machine) behaves the same as the git index, which stores LF.
#
# Shell scripts must keep LF. Command substitution preserves CR on a POSIX
# bash, so the pin comparison in scripts/env-check.sh:45 would compare
# "<sha>\r" against "<sha>" and never match. The dev machine's MSYS2 Git Bash
# strips the CR inside $(...), so it does not fail there today -- this rule is
# what keeps that difference from becoming a bug on another machine or a CI
# runner. Windows batch files want CRLF, which is what cmd.exe expects and what
# the file has historically shipped as.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# The pin file is read by scripts/fetch-torque3d.sh:14-19 and
# scripts/env-check.sh:42-45 through command substitution. Same CR hazard as
# the shell scripts above.
third_party/Torque3D.pin text eol=lf
